          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The construction of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            from a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
            (rigorously via
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %em renormalization
            is making a choice of fixing these ambiguities to produce a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Wightman76"}> Wightman et al. 76
            ).
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            the renormalization ambiguities are understood as the freedom of extending the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued distributions
            to the interaction points, in the sense of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Scharf 95
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Gauge+Theories+--+A+True+Ghost+Story"} Scharf 01
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization+theory"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization theory
            (theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeRenormalizationMainTheorem"}
            below) states that any two choices of such (re-)normalizations are uniquely related by a re-definition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            which introduces further point interactions of higher order (“counter terms”),
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            may naturally be organized via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+graphs"} Feynman graphs
            (
            %a{:href => "#GarciaBondiaLazzarini00"}> Garcia-Bondia &amp; Lazzarini 00
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Keller10"} Keller 10, chapter IV
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            defining the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is expressed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in renormalized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+graphs"} Feynman graphs
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            (
            %a{:href => "#Keller10"}> Keller 10 (IV.12)
            ).
          %p
            Historically the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            was motivated from intuition about the would-be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            as defining the result of the path integral without actually doing an integration over field configurations.
          %p
            But while
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            remains elusive, it has been shown that the (re-)normalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            thus constructed via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            is, at least under favorable circumstances, equivalently the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"}> Fedosov
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            induced by the given interacting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            (
            %a{:href => "#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ). This identifies the (re-)normalization freedom with the usual freedom in choosing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
          %p
            This also suggest that the construction of the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            ought to be given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 4
            is known. In particular the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenologically
            interesting case of a complete construction of interacting field theories on 4-dimensional spacetimes is presently unknown. For the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            this open problem is one of the “Millennium Problems” (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            The following is taken from
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} geometry of physics – A first idea of quantum field theory
          %h2#details Details
          %p There are different formulations of renormalization:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#InCausalPerturbationTheory"} In causal perturbation theory
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#BPHZRenormalization"} BPHZ and Hopf-Algebraic renormalization
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#OfTheoriesInBVForm"} Of theories in BV-CS form
          %h3#InCausalPerturbationTheory In causal perturbation theory
          %p
            We discuss (re-)normalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            in the rigorous formulation of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
          %p
            In this rigorous discussion no “infinite divergent quantities” (as in the original informal discussion due to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
            %em normalization
            of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            (as prominently highlighted in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "causal+perturbation+theoryscatt#Scharf95"} Scharf 95, see title, introduction, and section 4.3
          %ul
            %li
              %p Normalization
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em>
                      %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaserRenormalization"} Epstein-Glaser renormalization
                    \.
                %li
                  %p
                    %em>
                      %a{:href => "#UVRegularizationViaZ"} UV-Regularization via Counterterms
                    \.
                %li
                  %p
                    %em>
                      %a{:href => "#EffectiveQFTFlowWislonian"} Wilson-Polchinski effective QFT flow
                    \.
            %li
              %p Re-Normalization
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#SPRenormalizationGroup"} Stückelberg-Petermann re-normalization
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#RGFlowGeneral"} Renormalization group flow
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#ScalingTransformatinRGFlow"} Gell-Mann &amp; Low RG Flow
          %p
            The construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFTs"} perturbative QFTs
            may be explicitly described by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            to coinciding interaction points. This type of construction is called
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#EpsteinGlaserRenormalization"} Epstein-Glaser renormalization
              \.
          %p
            This inductive construction has the advantage that it gives accurate control over the space of available choices of renormalizations (theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExistenceRenormalization"}
            below) but it leaves the nature of the “new interactions” that are to be chosen at coinciding interaction points somwewhat implicit.
          %p
            Alternatively, one may
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+redefinition"} re-define the interactions
            explicitly (by adding “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"}> counterterms
            ”), depending on a chosen
            = succeed "-scale," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoff"} UV cutoff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            as the “cutoff is removed”. This is called (“re”-)normalization by
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#UVRegularizationViaZ"} UV-Regularization via Counterterms
              \.
          %p
            This still leaves open the question how to choose the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"} counterterms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+effective+action"} relative effective action
            induced by the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoff"} UV cutoff
            at any given cutoff scale. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
            change of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+effective+actions"} relative effective actions
            follows a universal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polchinski%27s+flow+equation"} Polchinski's flow equation
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FlowEquationPolchinski"}
            below). This makes the problem of (“re”-)normalization be that of solving this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            subject to chosen initial data. This is the perspective on (“re”-)normalization called
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#EffectiveQFTFlowWislonian"} Wilson-Polchinski effective QFT flow
              \.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization"} main theorem of perturbative renormalization
            states that different
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix+schemes"} S-matrix schemes
            are precisely related by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+redefinitions"} vertex redefinitions
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#SPRenormalizationGroup"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
              \.
          %p
            If a sub-collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+schemes"} renormalization schemes
            is parameterized by some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RG
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RG
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/main+theorem+of+perturbative+renormalization"} main theorem
            implies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+redefinitions"} vertex redefinitions
            depending on pairs of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi RG
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RG
            this is known as
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#RGFlowGeneral"} Renormalization group flow
          %p
            Specifically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+transformations"} scaling transformations
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            yields such a collection of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+schemes"} renormalization schemes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            is known as
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#ScalingTransformatinRGFlow"} Gell-Mann &amp; Low RG flow
              \.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h4#EpsteinGlaserRenormalization Epstein-Glaser normalization
          %p
            The construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbative quantum field theories
            around a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            is equivalently, by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "S-matrix#InteractingFieldAlgebraOfObservablesIsFormalDeformationQuantization"} this prop.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrices"} S-matrices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi int
                  %mo +
                  %mi j
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(g S_{int} + j A)
            in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            (
            %a{:href => "S-matrix#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}> this def.
            ) for the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g S_{int} + j A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RenormalizationIsInductivelyExtensionToDiagonal"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrices"} S-matrices
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductively
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            (remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TimeOrderedProductOfFixedInteraction"}
            and def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtensionOfDistributions"}
            below) of the corresponding
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            to the locus of coinciding interaction points (the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fat+diagonal"} fat diagonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            this way is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Epstein-Glaser+renormalization"} Epstein-Glaser-("re"-)normalization
            (
            %a{:href => "S-matrix#ExtensionOfTimeOrderedProoductsRenormalization"}> this def.
            ).
          %p
            By paying attention to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+degree+of+distributions"} scaling degree
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ScalingDegree"}
            below) one may precisely characterize the space of choices in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpaceOfPointExtensions"}
            below): For a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi int
                  %mo +
                  %mi j
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g S_{int} + j A
            it is inductively in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExistenceRenormalization"}
            below.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          #RenormalizationIsInductivelyExtensionToDiagonal.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalization
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
                to
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}', \Delta_H )
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge-fixed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              according to
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#VacuumFree"} this def.
            %p
              Assume that for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{T_{k}\}_{k \leq n}
              of arity
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \leq n
              have been constructed in the sense of
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#TimeOrderedProduct"} this def.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{n+1}
              of arity
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
              is uniquely fixed on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi diag
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Σ
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mi n
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo ∃
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo ≠
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Sigma^{n+1} \setminus diag(n)
                    \\;=\;
                    \\left\{
                    (x_i \in \Sigma)_{i = 1}^n
                    \\;\vert\;
                    \\underset{i,j}{\exists} (x_i \neq x_j)
                    \\right\}
            %p
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
              inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi diag
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \overset{diag}{\longrightarrow} \Sigma^{n}
              (where we regarded
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{n+1}
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+function"} generalized function
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^{n+1}
              according to
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#NotationForTimeOrderedProductsAsGeneralizedFunctions"} this remark
          %p
            This statement appears in (
            %a{:href => "#PopineauStora82"}> Popineau-Stora 82
            ), with (unpublished) details in (
            %a{:href => "#Stora93"}> Stora 93
            ), following personal communication by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Epstein"} Henri Epstein
            (according to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Duetsch18"} Dütsch 18, footnote 57
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen00"} Brunetti-Fredenhagen 00
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              We will construct an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^{n+1} \setminus \Sigma
              by subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi I
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_I \subset \Sigma^{n+1}
              which are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+unions"} disjoint unions
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} non-empty
              sets that are in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+order"} causal order
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+factorization"} causal factorization
              the time-ordered products
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{n+1}
              on these subsets are uniquely given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mi H
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{k}(-) \star_H T_{n-k}(-)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+of+unity"} partition of unity
            %p We now say this in detail:
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \subset \{1, \cdots, n+1\}
              write
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi I
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∖
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{I} \coloneqq \{1, \cdots, n+1\} \setminus I
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %mover
                        %mi I
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I, \overline{I} \neq \emptyset
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi I
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∨
                        %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                        %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mi j
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ∖
                          %mi I
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{C}_I
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\left\{
                    (x_i)_{i \in \{1, \cdots, n+1\}} \in \Sigma^{n+1}
                    \\;\vert\;
                    \\{x_i\}_{i \in I} {\vee\!\!\!\wedge} \{x_j\}_{j \in \{1, \cdots, n+1\} \setminus I}
                    \\right\}
                    \\;\subset\;
                    \\Sigma^{n+1}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since the
              = succeed "-relation" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+order"} causal order
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+future+cones"} closed future cones
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+past+cones"} closed past cones
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
              of the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo ∪
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mi I
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi I
                            %mo ,
                            %mover
                              %mi I
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo ≠
                            %mi ∅
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi I
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi diag
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\underset{ { I \subset \{1, \cdots, n+1\} \atop { I, \overline{I} \neq \emptyset } }   }{\cup}
                    \\mathcal{C}_I
                    \\;=\;
                    \\Sigma^{n+1} \setminus diag(\Sigma)
                    \\,.
            %p
              (Because any two distinct points in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetime"} globally hyperbolic spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              may be causally separated by a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            %p
              Hence the condition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+factorization"} causal factorization
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{n+1}
              implies that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction+of+distributions"} restricted
              to any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{I}
              these have to be given (in the condensed
              = succeed "-notation" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+function"} generalized function
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#NotationForTimeOrderedProductsAsGeneralizedFunctions"} this remark
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{X} = \{x_1, \cdots, x_{n+1}\} \in \mathcal{C}_I
              with corresponding induced tuples
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi I
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{I} \coloneqq \{x_i\}_{i \in I}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mover
                          %mi I
                          %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathbf{I}} \coloneqq \{x_i\}_{i \in \overline{I}}
              by
            #eq:InductiveIdentificationOfTimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AA
                    %mtext for
                    %mphantom
                      %mi A
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi I
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T_{n+1}( \mathbf{X} )
                    \\;=\;
                    T(\mathbf{I}) T(\overline{\mathbf{I}})
                    \\phantom{AA}
                    \\text{for}
                    \\phantom{A}
                    \\mathcal{X} \in \mathcal{C}_I
                    \\,.
            %p
              This shows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{n+1}
              is unique on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi diag
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^{n+1} \setminus diag(\Sigma)
              if it exists at all, hence if these local identifications glue to a global definition of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{n+1}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AA
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi I
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi I
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi ∅
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    I_1, I_2
                    \\subset
                    \\{1, \cdots, n+1\}
                    \\,,
                    \\phantom{AA}
                    I_1, I_2, \overline{I_1}, \overline{I_2} \neq \emptyset
                    \\,.
            %p By definition this implies that for
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi I
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi I
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{X} \in \mathcal{C}_{I_1} \cap \mathcal{C}_{I_2}
            %p a tuple of spacetime points which decomposes into causal order with respect to both these subsets, the corresponding mixed intersections of tuples are spacelike separated:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi I
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ¯
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mo &lt;
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi I
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{I}_1 \cap \overline{\mathbf{I}_2}
                    \\;
                    {\gt\!\!\!\!\lt}
                    \\;
                    \\overline{\mathbf{I}_1} \cap \mathbf{I}_2
                    \\,.
            %p
              By the assumption that the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ≠
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{T_k\}_{k \neq n}
              satisfy causal factorization, this implies that the corresponding time-ordered products commute:
            #eq:TimeOrderedProductsOfMixedIntersectionsCommute.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi I
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi I
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi I
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi I
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T(\mathbf{I}_1 \cap \overline{\mathbf{I}_2})
                    \\,
                    T(\overline{\mathbf{I}_1} \cap \mathbf{I}_2)
                    \\;=\;
                    T(\overline{\mathbf{I}_1} \cap \mathbf{I}_2)
                    \\,
                    T(\mathbf{I}_1 \cap \overline{\mathbf{I}_2})
                    \\,.
            %p
              Using this we find that the identifications of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{n+1}
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi I
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{I_1}
              and on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi I
                            %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{I_2}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:InductiveIdentificationOfTimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal"} (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi I
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi I
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{X}  \in \mathcal{C}_{I_1} \cap \mathcal{C}_{I_2}
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 1
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ∩
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ∩
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 2
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 1
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo ∩
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 1
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo ∩
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 2
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ∩
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %mi T
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mover
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                        %mi I
                                      %mn 1
                                  %mo ¯
                                %mo ∩
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mi T
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo ∩
                                %mover
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                        %mi I
                                      %mn 2
                                  %mo ¯
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 1
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo ∩
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 2
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi I
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi I
                                  %mn 2
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    T( \mathbf{I}_1 ) T( \overline{\mathbf{I}_1} )
                    \&amp; =
                    T( \mathbf{I}_1 \cap \mathbf{I}_2 )
                    T( \mathbf{I}_1 \cap \overline{\mathbf{I}_2} )
                    \\,
                    T( \overline{\mathbf{I}_1} \cap \mathbf{I}_2 )
                    T( \overline{\mathbf{I}_1} \cap \overline{\mathbf{I}_2} )
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    T( \mathbf{I}_1 \cap \mathbf{I}_2 )
                    \\underbrace{
                    T( \overline{\mathbf{I}_1} \cap \mathbf{I}_2 )
                    T( \mathbf{I}_1 \cap \overline{\mathbf{I}_2} )
                    }
                    T( \overline{\mathbf{I}_1} \cap \overline{\mathbf{I}_2} )
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    T( \mathbf{I}_2 )
                    T( \overline{\mathbf{I}_2} )
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              Here in the first step we expanded out the two factors using
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:InductiveIdentificationOfTimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal"} (1)
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi I
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_2
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TimeOrderedProductsOfMixedIntersectionsCommute"} (2)
              and in the last step we used again
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:InductiveIdentificationOfTimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal"} (1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_1
            %p To conclude, let
            #eq:PartitionCausalOfUnityForComplementOfDiagonal.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi I
                        %mo ∈
                        %msubsup
                          %mi C
                          %mi cp
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi Σ
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi supp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊂
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mi i
                        %mo )
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi I
                            %mo ⊂
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ⋯
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi I
                            %mo ,
                            %mover
                              %mi I
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo ≠
                            %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    \\chi_I
                    \\in
                    C^\infty_{cp}(\Sigma^{n+1}),
                    \\,
                    supp(\chi_I) \subset \mathcal{C}_i
                    \\right)_{ { I \subset \{1, \cdots, n+1\} } \atop { I, \overline{I} \neq \emptyset } }
            %p
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+of+unity"} partition of unity
              subordinate to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              formed by the
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_I
            %p
              Then the above implies that setting for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi diag
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{X} \in \Sigma^{n+1} \setminus diag(\Sigma)
            #eq:TimeOrderedProductsAwayFromDiagonalByInduction.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi I
                            %mo ∈
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ⋯
                            %mo ,
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi I
                            %mo ,
                            %mover
                              %mi I
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo ≠
                            %mi ∅
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T_{n+1}(\mathbf{X})
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\underset{
                    { I \in \{1, \cdots, n+1\} }
                    \\atop
                    { I, \overline{I} \neq \emptyset }
                    }{\sum}
                    \\chi_i(\mathbf{X}) T( \mathbf{I} ) T( \overline{\mathbf{I}} )
            %p is well defined and satisfies causal factorization.
          #TimeOrderedProductOfFixedInteraction.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
                of fixed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                as
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}', \Delta_H )
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge-fixed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              according to
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#VacuumFree"} this def.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+vector+bundle"} trivial vector bundle
              (
              %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#TrivialVectorBundleAsAFieldBundle"}> this example
              )
            %p and let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi LoObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g S_{int} + j A
                    \\;\in\;
                    LoObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})[ [ \hbar, g, j] ]\langle g,j\rangle
            %p
              be a polynomial
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local observable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatically switched
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi L
                          %mrow
                            %mi int
                            %mo ,
                            %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi α
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ′
                        %mo ∈
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi Σ
                          %mrow
                            %mi p
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mtext BV-BRST
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left\{
                    \\mathbf{L}_{int,i}, \mathbf{\alpha}_{i'} \in \Omega^{p+1,0}_\Sigma(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})
                    \\right\}_{i,i'}
            %p
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+densities"} Lagrangian densities
              which are monomials in the field and jet coordinates, and a corresponding finite set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi sw
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %msubsup
                        %mi C
                        %mi cp
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mrow
                          %mi sw
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                          %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %msubsup
                        %mi C
                        %mi cp
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left\{
                    g_{sw,i} \in C^\infty_{cp}(\Sigma)\langle g \rangle
                    \\,,\,
                    j_{sw,i'} \in C^\infty_{cp}(\Sigma)\langle j \rangle
                    \\right\}
            %p
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switchings"} adiabatic switchings
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi sw
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mi int
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo +
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ′
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mrow
                          %mi sw
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                          %mo ′
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi α
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ′
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g S_{int} + j A
                    \\;=\;
                    \\tau_{\Sigma}
                    \\left(
                    \\underset{i}{\sum}
                    g_{sw,i} \mathbf{L}_{int,i}
                    \\;+\;
                    \\underset{i'}{\sum}
                    j_{sw,i'} \mathbf{\alpha}_{i'}
                    \\right)
            %p
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression+of+variational+differential+forms"} transgression of variational differential forms
              (
              %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#TransgressionOfVariationalDifferentialFormsToConfigrationSpaces"}> this def.
              ) of the sum of the products of these
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatic switching
              with these
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+densities"} Lagrangian densities
            %p
              In order to discuss the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(g S_{int} + j A)
              and hence the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              of the special form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %mi g
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi int
                            %mo +
                            %mi j
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ⋯
                            %mo ,
                            %mi g
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi int
                            %mo +
                            %mi j
                            %mi A
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mtext factors
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_k\left(  \underset{k \, \text{factors}}{\underbrace{g S_{int} + j A, \cdots, g S_{int} + j A }} \right)
              it is sufficient to restrict attention to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction"} restriction
              of each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_k
              to the subspace of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
              induced by the finite set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+densities"} Lagrangian densities
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mi int
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi α
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ′
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                          %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\mathbf{L}_{int,i}, \mathbf{\alpha}_{i'}\}_{i,i'}
            %p
              This restriction is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functional"} continuous linear functional
              on the corresponding space of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+functions"} bump functions
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi sw
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mrow
                          %mi sw
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                          %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{g_{sw,i}, j_{sw,i'}\}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} dstributional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
              of a corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+vector+bundle"} trivial vector bundle
            %p
              In terms of this, prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RenormalizationIsInductivelyExtensionToDiagonal"}
              says that the choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_k
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductively
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              a choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
              to the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = \mathbb{R}^{p,1}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
              and we impose the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+condition"} renormalization condition
              “translation invariance” (
              %a{:href => "S-matrix#RenormalizationConditions"}> this def.
              ) then each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_k
              is a distribution on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^{k-1} = \mathbb{R}^{(p+1)(k-1)}
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
              is from the complement of the origina
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in \mathbb{R}^{(p+1)(k-1)}
          %p
            Therefore we now discuss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtensionOfDistributions"}
            below) on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            from the complement of the origin to the origin. Since the space of choices of such extensions turns out to depend on the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+degree+of+distributions"} scaling degree of distributions
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ScalingDegree"}
            below).
          #RescaledDistribution.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+degree+of+distributions"}> rescaled distribution
                )
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \in (0,\infty) \subset \mathbb{R}
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
              write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi s
                                  %mi λ
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi n
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mi λ
                            %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbb{R}^n
                    \&amp;\overset{s_\lambda}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    \\mathbb{R}^n
                    \\\
                    x &amp;\mapsto&amp; \lambda x
                    }
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
              given by multiplication with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real vector space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %p
              Then for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^n)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              the
              %em rescaled distribution
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+a+distribution"} pullback
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_\lambda
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi u
                      %mi λ
                    %mo ≔
                    %msubsup
                      %mi s
                      %mi λ
                      %mo *
                    %mi u
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    u_\lambda
                    \\coloneqq
                    s_\lambda^\ast u
                    \\;\in\;
                    \\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^n)
                    \\,.
            %p Explicitly, this is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi 𝒟
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
