                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{Z}_{\Lambda}(g S_{int} + j A)
                    \\;=\;
                    g S_{int} + j A
                    \\;+\;
                    S_{counter,\Lambda}
                    \\,.
            %p
              In this language prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UVRegularization"}
              says that for every free field vacuum and every choice of local interaction, there is a choice of counterterms to the interaction that defines a corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalized
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} (re"-)normalized
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              arises from some choice of counterterms.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h4#EffectiveQFTFlowWislonian Wilson-Polchinski effective QFT flow
          %p
            We have seen
            %a{:href => "#UVRegularizationViaZ"} above
            that a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoff"} UV cutoff
            induces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+S-matrix+schemes"} effective S-matrix schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}_\Lambda
            at cutoff scale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            (def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SMatrixEffective"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+effective+actions"} relative effective actions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi eff
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{eff,\Lambda}
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EffectiveActionRelative"}
            below) which are such that their effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            at scale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            coincide with the true scattering amplitudes of a genuine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observable"} local
            interaction as the cutoff is removed. This is the Wilsonian picture of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            at a given cutoff scale (remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#pQFTEffective"}
            below). Crucially the “flow” of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+effective+actions"} relative effective actions
            with the cutoff scale satisfies a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            that in itself is independent of the full UV-theory; this is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polchinski%27s+flow+equation"} Polchinski's flow equation
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FlowEquationPolchinski"}
            below). Solving this equation for given choice of initial value data is hence another way of choosing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalization
            constants.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
