                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            – called the
            %strong
              (dimensional)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regularization+%28physics%29"} regularization
              parameter
            –
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Amplitude
                  %mo :
                  %mi CertainGraphs
                  %mo →
                  %mi LaurentPolynomials
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Amplitude : CertainGraphs \to LaurentPolynomials
          %p
            and wishes to extract a “meaningful” finite component when evaluated at vanishing regularization parameter
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = 0
          %p
            A prescription – called
            %strong renormalization scheme
            – for adding to a given amplitude in a certain recursive fashion further terms – called
            %strong counterterms
            – such that the resulting modified amplitude – called the
            %strong renormalized amplitude
            – is finite at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z=0
            was once given by physicists and is called the
            %strong BPHZ-procedure
            \.
          %p This procedure justifies itself mainly through the remarkable fact that the numbers obtained from it match certain numbers measured in particle accelerators to fantastic accuracy.
          %p
            %strong Its combinatorial Hopf-algebraic interpretation
          %p The combinatorial Hopf algebraic approach to perturbative quantum field theory, see for instance
          %ul
            %li
              Hector Figueroa,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jose+Gracia-Bondia"} Jose Gracia-Bondia
              %em Combinatorial Hopf algebras in quantum field theory I
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0408145"}> arXiv
              ),
          %p starts with the observation that the BPHZ-procedure can be understood
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                by noticing that there is secretly a natural
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                structure on the collection of amplitudes;
            %li
              %p
                which is induced from the fact that there is secretly a natural
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
                structure on the vector space whose basis consists of graphs;
            %li
              %p
                and with respect to which the BPHZ-procedure is simply the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Birkhoff+decomposition"} Birkhoff decomposition
                of group valued functions on the circle into a divergent and a finite part.
          %p
            The Hopf algebra structure on the vector space whose basis consists of graphs can be understood most conceptually in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-Lie+algebra"} pre-Lie algebras
          %p
            %strong The Connes-Kreimer theorem
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Birkhoff+decomposition"} Birkhoff decomposition
            of a loop
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : S^1 \to G
            in a complex group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a continuation of the loop to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_+
                on the standard disk inside the circle;
            %li
              %p
                a holomorphic function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_-
                on the complement of this disk in the projective
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
            %li
              %p such that on the unit circle the original loop is reproduced as
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo +
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\phi = \phi_+ \cdot (\phi_-)^{-1}
                      \\,,
              %p
                with the product and the inverse on the right taken in the group
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %p
                Notice that by the assumption of holomorphicity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_+(0)
                is a well defined element of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Connes-Kreimer)
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                  is the group of
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/character"} character
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo =
                        %mi Hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi H
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        G = Hom(H,\mathbb{C})
                %p then the Birkhoff decomposition always exists and is given by the formula
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo −
                        %mo :
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi Counit
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo −
                        %mi PolePartOf
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Product
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo −
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mi Counit
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi Coproduct
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\phi_- :  (X \in H) \mapsto Counit(X)
                        \-
                        PolePartOf(
                        Product(\phi_- \otimes \phi)
                        \\circ (1 \otimes (1 - Counit))
                        \\circ Coproduct (X)
                        )
                        \\,.
              %li
                %p
                  There is naturally the structure of a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi H
                          %mo =
                          %mi Graphs
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = Graphs
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              %li
                %p The BPHZ renormalization-procedure for amplitudes is nothing but the first item applied to the special case of the second item.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_21 Proof
            %p The proof is given in
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"} Dirk Kreimer
                %em Renormalization in quantum field theory I
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912092"}> arXiv
                )
          %p
            %strong The Hopf-algebra perspective on QFT
          %p
            This result first of all makes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            an organizational principle for (re-)expressing familiar operations in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            Computing the renormalization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_+
            of an amplitude
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            amounts to using the above formula to compute the counterterm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_-
            and then evaluating the right hand side of
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo +
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mrow
                      %mi renormalized
                      %mi amplitude
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mi amplitude
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mrow
                      %mi convolution
                      %mi product
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo −
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mi counterterm
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underbrace{\phi_+}_{renormalized amplitude} = \underbrace{\phi}_{amplitude} \underbrace{\cdot}_{convolution product} \underbrace{\phi_-}_{counterterm}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the product is the group product on characters, hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+product"} convolution product
            of characters.
          %p
            Every elegant reformulation has in it the potential of going beyond mere reformulation by allowing to see structures invisible in a less natural formulation. For instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"} Dirk Kreimer
            %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/03/recent_developments_in_quantum.html#c010903"} claims
            that the Hopf algebra language allows him to see patterns in perturbative quantum gravity previously missed.
          %p
            %strong Gauge theory and BV-BRST with Hopf algebra
          %p
            Walter von Suijlekom is thinking about the Hopf-algebraic formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+theory"} BRST-BV methods
            in nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
          %p In his nicely readable
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Walter von Suijlekom
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Walter+von+Suijlekom"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalization of gauge fields using Hopf algebra
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0801.3170"} arXiv
          %p
            he reviews the central idea: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST"} BRST
            formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            manifests itself at the level of the resulting
            %em bare
            i.e. unnormalized amplitudes in certain relations satisfied by these, the
            %strong Slavnov-Taylor identities
            \.
          %p Renormalization of gauge theories is consistent only if these relations are still respected by renormalized amplitudes, too. We can reformulate this in terms of Hopf algebra now:
          %p
            the relations between amplitudes to be preserved under renormalization must define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+ideal"} Hopf ideal
            in the Hopf algebra of graphs.
          %p
            Walter von Suijlekom proves this to be the case for Slavnov-Taylor in his
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/arxiv/pdf/0801/0801.3170v1.pdf#page=12"} theorem 9 on p. 12
          %p
            As a payoff, he obtains a very transparent way to prove the generalization of
            %strong Dyson’s formula
            to nonabelian gauge theory, which expresses renormalized Green’s functions in terms of unrenormalized Green’s functions “at bare coupling”. This is his
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/arxiv/pdf/0801/0801.3170v1.pdf#page=12"} corollary 12 on p. 13
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+theory"} BRST-BV quantization
            these statements are subsumed, he says, by the structure encoded in the Hopf ideal which corresponds to imposing the BV-master equation. See also (
            %a{:href => "#Suijlekom"}> Suijlekom
            ).
          %h3#lattice_renormalization Lattice renormalization
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+renormalization"} lattice renormalization
          %h3#OfTheoriesInBVForm Of theories in BV-CS form
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello 07
            ) a renormalization procedure is discussed that applies to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            that are given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            which can be given in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S(\phi)
                  \=
                  \\langle \phi , Q \phi \rangle
                  +
                  I(\phi)
          %p where
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                are sections of a graded
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                on which
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
                a compatible
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antibracket"} antibracket
                pairing such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,Q, \langle \rangle)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
                (as discussed there) in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                that is at least cubic.
          %p
            These are action functionals that are well adapted to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            and for which there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra+of+observables"} factorization algebra of observables
          %p
            Most of the fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            are of this form, notably
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomials"} invariant polynomials
            are perturbatively of this form, notably ordinary 3d
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            For a discussion of just the simple special case of 3d CS see (
            %a{:href => "#Costello11"}> Costello 11, chapter 5.4 and 5.14
            ).
          %p
            For comparison of the following with other renormalization schemes, see at (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello 07, section 1.7
            ).
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#TheSetup"} The setup
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#OperatorKernelsAndPropagators"} Operator (heat) kernels and propagators
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#TheRenormalizationGroupOperator"} The renormalization group operator
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ThePathIntegral"} The path integral
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#RenormalizedAction"} Renormalized action
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Renormalization"} Renormalization
          %p#TheSetup
            %strong The setup
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_9 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo .
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \langle, -,-\rangle. Q)
            %p
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"} kinematics
              \:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  (“
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"}> spacetime
                  ”/“
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"}> worldvolume
                  ”);
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℤ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} graded
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X
                  (the “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"}> field bundle
                  ” containing also in general
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antifields"} antifields
                  and
                  = succeed ");" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghosts"} ghosts
              %li
                %p
                  equipped with a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
                  (the “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antibracket"} antibracket
                  = succeed "\u201D)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mi loc
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi E
                        %mo ×
                        %mi E
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi Dens
                          %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\langle -,-\rangle_{loc} \;\colon\; E \times E \to Dens_X
                %p
                  from the fiberwise
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  with itself to the compex
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+bundle"} density bundle
                  which is fiberwise
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p non-degenerate
                  %li
                    %p anti-symmetric
                  %li
                    %p of degree -1
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi cp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}_c \coloneqq \Gamma_{cp}(E)
              for the space of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle -,-\rangle
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathcal{E}_c
                    \\otimes
                    \\mathcal{E}_c
                    \\to
                    \\mathbb{C}
            %p for the induced pairing on sections
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi loc
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle \phi, \psi\rangle
                    \=
                    \\int_{x \in X} \langle \phi(x), \psi(x)\rangle_{loc}
                    \\,.
            %p
              The paring being non-degenerate means that we have an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi Dens
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} E^* \otimes Dens_X
              and we write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo !
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi Dens
                      %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    E^! \coloneqq E^* \otimes Dens_X
                    \\,.
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operator"} differential operator
                  on sections of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi ℰ
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        Q \;\colon\; \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{C}
                %p of degree 1 such that
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℰ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E}, Q)
                      is an
                      = succeed ";" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+complex"} elliptic complex
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Q
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                      is
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+operator"} self-adjoint
                      with respect to
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
                      in that for all
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ψ
                            %mo ∈
                            %msub
                              %mi ℰ
                              %mi c
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi,\psi \in \mathcal{E}_c
                      of homogeneous degree we have
                      = succeed "." do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi Q
                              %mi ψ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                              %mo =
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                  %mi ϕ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                              %mi Q
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi ψ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \phi , Q \psi\rangle = (-1)^{{\vert \phi\vert}} \langle Q \phi, \psi\rangle
            %p From this data we obtain:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi ℰ
                          %mi c
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \colon \mathcal{E}_c \to \mathbb{C}
                  of this corresponding free field theory is
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ↦
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Q
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        S \;\colon\; \phi \mapsto \int_X \langle \phi, Q \phi\rangle
                        \\,.
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+BV-complex"} classical BV-complex
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+algebra"} symmetric algebra
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sym
                        %msup
                          %mi ℰ
                          %mo !
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym  \mathcal{E}^!
                  of sections of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi E
                          %mo !
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^!
                  equipped with the induced action of the differential
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                  and the pairing
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi α
                        %mo ,
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ≔
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\{\alpha,\beta\} \coloneqq  \int_{x \in X} \langle \alpha(x), \beta(x)\rangle
                        \\,.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_10 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \in \cdots
            %p (…)
          #LaplaceOperatorFromGaugeFixing.num_defn
            %h6#definition_11 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing+operator"} gauge fixing operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Q
                      %mi GF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q^{GF}
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Q
                      %mi GF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H \coloneqq [Q, Q^{GF}]
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Laplace+operator"} generalized Laplace operator
          %p#OperatorKernelsAndPropagators
            %strong Operator (heat) kernels and propagators
          #ConvolutionOfOperatorKernel.num_defn
            %h6#definition_12 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi K
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = \sum K' \otimes K'' \in \mathcal{E} \otimes \mathcal{E}
              the corresponding
              %strong convolution operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \star \colon \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi K
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K \star e  = (-1)^{\vert e\vert} \sum K' \otimes \langle K'', e\rangle
                    \\,.
          #HeatKernel.num_defn
            %h6#definition_13 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \colon \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} linear operator
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heat+kernel"} heat kernel
              for it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{(-)} \colon \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0} \to \mathcal{E}\otimes \mathcal{E}
              such that for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0}
              the convolution with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_t
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConvolutionOfOperatorKernel"}
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cdotH
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\cdotH
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi t
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo =
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi t
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K_t \star = \exp(-t H) \;\;\;\;\; \forall t \in \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0}
                    \\,.
          #UniquenessOfHeatKernel.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_16 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Laplace+operator"} generalized Laplace operator
              such as the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Q
                      %mi GF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [Q,Q^{GF}]
              of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LaplaceOperatorFromGaugeFixing"}
              there is a unique
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heat+kernel"} heat kernel
              which is moreover a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          #DifferentialOperatorByKernel.num_defn
            %h6#definition_14 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Sym
                      %mn 2
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi =  \sum \phi' \otimes \phi'' \in Sym^2 \mathcal{E}
              write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi ϕ
                    %mo ≔
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ″
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ′
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial_{\phi}
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\frac{1}{2}
                    \\sum \partial_{\phi''} \partial_{\phi'}
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello 07, p. 32
            ).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_17 Proposition
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi ϕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                        %mi ϕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [\partial_\phi, Q] = \partial_{Q \phi}
                    \\,.
          %p#TheRenormalizationGroupOperator
            %strong The renormalization group operator
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_15 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Q
                      %mi GF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q^{GF}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing+operator"} gauge fixing operator
              of def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LaplaceOperatorFromGaugeFixing"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_t
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heat+kernel"} heat kernel
              of the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Laplace+operator"} generalized Laplace operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Q
                      %mi GF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = [Q, Q^{GF}]
              by prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniquenessOfHeatKernel"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon, T \in \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Q
                      %mi GF
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi t
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi d
                    %mi t
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    P(\epsilon, T)
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\int_{\epsilon}^T
                    (Q^{GF} \otimes 1)
                    K_t \; d t
                    \\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\in \mathcal{E} \otimes \mathcal{E}\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello 07, p. 33
            )
          #RenormalizationOperator.num_defn
            %h6#definition_16 Definition
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mi log
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi ℏ
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %mi P
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ϵ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo )
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Gamma(P(\epsilon,T), S)
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\hbar log
                    \\left(
                    \\exp\left(\hbar \partial_{P(\epsilon,T)}\right)
                    \\exp\left(I/\hbar\right)
                    \\right)
                    \\;\;
                    \\in \mathcal{O}(\mathcal{E}, \mathbb{C}[ [ \hbar ] ])
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_{\cdots}
              is given by def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DifferentialOperatorByKernel"}
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello 07, def. 6.6.1
            )
          %p#ThePathIntegral
            %strong The path integral
          #PathIntegralByLimitInDimension0.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_18 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = *
              is the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              exists as an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
              and is equal to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mi log
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Im
                        %msup
                          %mi Q
                          %mi GF
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi ℝ
                    %mi exp
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 1
                          %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo +
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi d
                      %mi μ
                      %mo )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\hbar log
                    \\int_{x \in (Im Q^{GF})_{\mathbb{R}}}
                    \\exp\left(
                    \\tfrac{1}{2} \langle x, Q x\rangle
                    +
                    I(x + a) d \mu
                    \\right)
                    \=
                    \\Gamma(P(0,\infty), I)(a)
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello 07, lemma 6.6.2
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of positive
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\underset{\epsilon \to 0}{\lim}
                    \\Gamma(P(\epsilon,\infty), I)(a)
            %p
              does not in general exist. Renormalization is the process of adding
              = succeed "-corrections" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"} counterterms
              – such as to make it exist after all. In this case we may regard the limit, by prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PathIntegralByLimitInDimension0"}
              %em definition
              of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
          %p#RenormalizedAction
            %strong Renormalized action
          #RenormalizedActionAndCounterterms.num_defn
            %h6#definition_17 Definition
            %p
              Given the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ↦
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mi phi
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo +
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \colon \phi \mapsto \frac{1}{2}\langle \phi, Q phi\rangle + I(\phi)
              %strong renormalization
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo &gt;
                            %mn 0
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mo ≥
                            %mi k
                    %msup
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mi i
                    %msubsup
                      %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                      %mi CT
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    I^R(\hbar, \epsilon)
                    \=
                    I(\hbar)
                    \-
                    \\underset{{i \gt 0} \atop  { k \geq k}}{\sum}
                    \\hbar^i I^{CT}_{i,k}(\epsilon)
            %p
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\underset{\epsilon \to 0}{\lim}
                    \\Gamma(P(\epsilon,T), I^R(\epsilon))
                    \\,,
            %p
              by def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RenormalizationOperator"}
          %p
            The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                    %mi CT
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{CT}_{i,k}
            are called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"} counterterms
          %p#Renormalization
            %strong Renormalization
          #RenormalizationScheme.num_defn
            %h6#definition_18 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} renormalization scheme
              is a decomposition of functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mrow
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A} \simeq \mathcal{A}_{\geq 0} \oplus \mathcal{A}_{\gt 0}
            %p
              such that the functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in \mathcal{A}_{\geq 0}
              are non-singular in that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{\epsilon \to 0}{\lim} f(\epsilon)
              exists.
          %p
            Hence this is a choice of picking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            in functions that are not necessarily defined at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon = 0
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              Given any choice of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} renormalization scheme
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RenormalizationScheme"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"} counterterms
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msubsup
                        %mi I
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo ,
                          %mi i
                        %mi CT
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{I^{CT}_{k,i}\}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RenormalizedActionAndCounterterms"}
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  each counterterm is in the chosen
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒜
                          %mrow
                            %mo &gt;
                            %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}_{\gt 0}
                  as a function of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ϵ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \gt 0
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germ"} germ
                  of a counterterm at
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                  depends only on the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/germ"} germ
                  of the given field theory data
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi E
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Q
                        %mo ,
                        %msup
                          %mi Q
                          %mi GF
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, Q, Q^{GF})
                  at that point.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello 07, theorem B, p. 38
            ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_19 Definition
            %p
              Given a renormalization
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{R}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ht
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \in \mathbb{R}_{\ht 0}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi CT
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Gamma^R(P(0,T), I)
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\underset{\epsilon \to 0}{\to}
                    \\Gamma(P(\epsilon, R), I - I^{CT}(\epsilon))
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              We think of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma^R(P(0,T), I)
              as the renormalized
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
              of the original action at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_19 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Gamma^R(P(0,T'), I)
                    \=
                    \\Gamma(P(T,T'), \; \Gamma^R(P(0,T), I ))
            %p holds.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Costellon"}> Costello 07, lemma 9.0.6
            ).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#vacuum_energy_and_cosmological_constant Vacuum energy and Cosmological constant
          %p
            The renormalization freedom in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"}> perturbative quantum gravity
            ) induces freedom in the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+value"} vacuum expectation value
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            and hence in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %p
            For details see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "cosmological+constant#InPerturbativeQuantumGravity"} there
          %h3#chernsimons_level Chern-Simons level
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Chern-Simons+level#LevelRenormalization"} Chern-Simons level renormalization
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group"}> renormalization group
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+scheme"} renormalization scheme
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field-strength+renormalization"} field-strength renormalization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalon"} renormalon
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regularization+%28physics%29"} regularization (physics)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+quantization"} BV quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"}> gauge fixing
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gribov+ambiguity"} Gribov ambiguity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+Galois+group"} cosmic Galois group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+renormalization"} differential renormalization
          %h2#References References
          %p
            After the original informal suggestions by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
          %ul
            %li#Dyson49
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freeman+Dyson"}> Freeman Dyson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The raditation theories of Tomonaga, Schwinger and Feynman
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://web.ihep.su/dbserv/compas/src/dyson49b/eng.pdf"} pdf
          %p the mathematics of renormalization was finally understood and summarized in the 1975 Erice Majorana School:
          %ul
            %li#Wightman76
              G. Velo and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Wightman"} Arthur Wightman
              (eds.)
              %em Renormalization Theory
              Proceedings of the 1975 Erice summer school, NATO ASI Series C 23, D. Reidel, Dordrecht, 1976
          %p
            which included
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPHZ+renormalization"} BPHZ renormalization
            %span.newWikiWord
              forest formula
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/forest+formula"} ?
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            method for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
          %p
            Little advancement happened until the identification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            structure in the
            %span.newWikiWord
              forest formula
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/forest+formula"} ?
            due to
          %ul
            %li#Kreimer97
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"}> Dirk Kreimer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Hopf algebra structure of perturbative quantum field theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9707029"} q-alg/9707029
          %p
            This finally triggered the formulation of
            %span.newWikiWord
              `causal perturbation theory
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%60causal+perturbation+theory"} ?
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            in
          %ul
            %li#GarciaBondiaLazzarini00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jose+Gracia-Bondia"}> Jose Gracia-Bondia
                , S. Lazzarini,
                %em Connes-Kreimer-Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0006106"}> arXiv:hep-th/0006106
                )
            %li#Keller10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kai+Keller"}> Kai Keller
                , chapter IV of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dimensional Regularization in Position Space and a Forest Formula for Regularized Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.2148"} arXxiv:1006.2148
            %li#DuetschFredenhagenKellerRejzner14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kai+Keller"} Kai Keller
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dimensional Regularization in Position Space, and a Forest Formula for Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
              %p
                55(12), 122303 (2014) (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.5424"}> arXiv:1311.5424
                )
          %p
            Thus the original “dark art” of the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            via renormalization finds a complete rigorous formulation.
          %p In
          %ul
            %li#Collini16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Collini"}> Giovanni Collini
              ,
              %em Fedosov Quantization and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09626"}> arXiv:1603.09626
              )
          %p
            it is shown that (at least under favorable circumstances) the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            is equivalently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov deformation quantization
            of the given interacting
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
          %h3#General Review
          %p General monograph:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Manfred+Salmhofer"}> Manfred Salmhofer
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalization – An Introduction
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-03873-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-662-03873-4
          %p
            Monograph using
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
          %ul
            %li#Duetsch18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              , chapters 3 and 4 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/From+classical+field+theory+to+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} From classical field theory to perturbative quantum field theory
          %p Informal introductions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                G. Peter Lepage,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What is Renormalization?
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0506330"} arXiv:hep-ph/0506330
            %li#Weinberg09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Weinberg"}> Steven Weinberg
                ,
                %em Effective Field Theory, Past and Future
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0908.1964"}> arXiv:0908.1964
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+E.+Borcherds"}> R. E. Borcherds
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Renormalization and quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1008.0129"} arxiv/1008.0129
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arnold+Neumaier"}> Arnold Neumaier
                ,
                %em Renormalizatin without infinities – an elementary tutorial
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~neum/ms/ren.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p Further review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Bertrand Delamotte:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A hint of renormalization
                %strong 72
                (2004) 170-184 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0212049"}> arXiv:hep-th/0212049
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1119/1.1624112"} doi:10.1119/1.1624112
            %li
              %p
                V. Mastropietro,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Renormalization: general theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+Mathematical+Physics+2nd+ed"} Encyclopedia of Mathematical Physics 2nd ed
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11400"} arXiv:2312.11400
            %li
              %p
                Leonardo Di Giustino:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Renormalisation
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+Particle+Physics"} Encyclopedia of Particle Physics
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.02005"}> arXiv:2511.02005
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On application to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+matter+physics"} condensed matter physics
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Manfred+Salmhofer"}> Manfred Salmhofer
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalization in Condensed Matter: Fermionic Systems – from Mathematics to Materials
              %strong 941
              (2019) 868-899 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01766"}> arXiv:1807.01766
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2018.07.004"} doi:10.1016/j.nuclphysb.2018.07.004
          %p
            More informal (and historical) review by and for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy+of+physics"} philosophers of physics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Tian Yu Cao,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Silvan+S.+Schweber"} Silvan S. Schweber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The conceptual foundations and the philosophical aspects of renormalization theory
                %strong 97
                (1993) 33–108 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01255832"}> doi:10.1007/BF01255832
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"}> Jeremy Butterfield
                , Nazim Bouatta:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Renormalization for Philosophers
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Metaphysics in Contemporary Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.4532"} arXiv:1406.4532
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1163/9789004310827_018"} doi:10.1163/9789004310827_018
            %li
              %p
                Karen Crowther, Niels Linnemann:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Renormalizability, fundamentality and a final theory: The role of UV-completion in the search for quantum gravity
                %strong 70
                2 (2019) 377-406 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/bjps/axx052"}> doi:10.1093/bjps/axx052
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.06777"} arXiv:1705.06777
          %h3#in_causal_perturbation_theory_2 In causal perturbation theory
          %p The idea of Epstein-Glaser renormalization is due to
          %ul
            %li#EpsteinGlaser73
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Epstein"}> Henri Epstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Glaser"} Vladimir Glaser
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Role+of+locality+in+perturbation+theory"} The Role of locality in perturbation theory
          %p following precursors in
          %ul
            %li#StueckelbergPetermann53
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Petermann"} André Petermann
                = succeed "," do
                  %em La normalisation des constants dans la theorie des quanta
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (see also
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/St%C3%BCckelberg-Petermann+renormalization+group"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
            %li#BogoliubovShirkov76
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolay+Bogoliubov"}> Nikolay Bogoliubov
                ,
                = succeed ",," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Shirkov"} Dmitry Shirkov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to the Theory of Quantized Fiels
          %p
            This was formulated in terms of splittings of distributions. The equivalent formulation in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensions+of+distributions"} extensions of distributions
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#PopineauStora82
              %p
                G. Popineau and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raymond+Stora"} Raymond Stora
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A pedagogical remark on the main theorem of perturbative renormalization theory
            %li#Stora93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raymond+Stora"}> Raymond Stora
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Differential algebras in Lagrangean field theory
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagen00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"} arXiv:math-ph/9903028
          %p Exposition includes
          %ul
            %li#Brouder10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Brouder"}> Christian Brouder
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Multiplication of distributions
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BrouderProductOfDistributions.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/St%C3%BCckelberg-Petermann+renormalization+group"} Stückelberg-Petermann renormalization group
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#StueckelbergPetermann53
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+St%C3%BCckelberg"}> Ernst Stückelberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Petermann"} André Petermann
              = succeed "," do
                %em La normalisation des constantes dans la theorie des quanta
          %p
            The relation of Epstein-Glaser/Stückelberg-Petermann to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+group+flow"} renormalization group flow
            of
          %ul
            %li#GellMannLow54
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Murray+Gell-Mann"} Murray Gell-Mann
              and F. E. Low,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum Electrodynamics at Small Distances
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.fafnir.phyast.pitt.edu/py3765/GellManLow.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            of
          %ul
            %li#Wilson71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Wilson"}> Kenneth Wilson
                ,
                %em Renormalization group and critical phenomena
                , I., Physical review B 4(9) (1971).
            %li#Polchinski84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Polchinski"}> Joseph Polchinski
                ,
                %em Renormalization and effective Lagrangians
                , Nuclear Phys. B B231, 1984 (
                %a{:href => "http://max2.physics.sunysb.edu/~rastelli/2016/Polchinski.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p is due to
          %ul
            %li#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2038"} arXiv:0901.2038
            %li#Duetsch10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Connection between the renormalization groups of Stückelberg-Petermann and Wilson
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.5604"} arXiv:1012.5604
            %li#DuetschFredenhagenKellerRejzner14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kai+Keller"} Kai Keller
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dimensional Regularization in Position Space, and a Forest Formula for Epstein-Glaser Renormalization
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.5424"} arXiv:1311.5424
          %p Review is in
          %ul
            %li#Duetsch18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              , section 3.8 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/From+classical+field+theory+to+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} From classical field theory to perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "perturbation+theory#ReferencesInAQFT"} perturbation theory – In AQFT
          %p
            Applications to the renormalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            on curved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
          %ul
            %li#Hollands07
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalized Quantum Yang-Mills Fields in Curved Spacetime
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.3340"} arXiv:0705.3340
          %p Relating Epstein-Glaser renormalization (causal perturbation theory) to the Connes-Kreimer formulation of BPHZ renormalization:
          %ul
            %li
              Jonah Epstein, Arne Hofmann,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Prinz"} David Prinz
              %em Multiplicative Renormalization in Causal Perturbation Theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.09918"}> arXiv:2512.09918
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#bphz_renormalization BPHZ Renormalization
          %p BPHZ renormalization was introduced in particular in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Hepp"}> Klaus Hepp
              ,
              %em Théorie de la Renormalisation
              Lect. Notes in Phys. Springer (1969)
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Klaus Sibold,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bogoliubov-Parasiuk-Hepp-Zimmermann renormalization scheme
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4249/scholarpedia.7306"} doi:10.4249/scholarpedia.7306
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noncommutative+Geometry%2C+Quantum+Fields+and+Motives"} Noncommutative Geometry, Quantum Fields and Motives
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Talagrand"}> Michel Talagrand
                , §16 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What is a Quantum Field Theory? – A first Introduction for Mathematicians
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108225144"} doi:10.1017/9781108225144
          %p The original articles on the Hopf-algebraic formulation:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"} Dirk Kreimer
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalization in quantum field theory and the Riemann-Hilbert problem. I. The Hopf algebra structure of graphs and the main theorem
              %strong 210
              (2000), no. 1, 249–273,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912092"} hep-th/9912092
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1748177"} MR2002f:81070
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s002200050779"} doi
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  II. The
                  = succeed "-function," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi β
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
              %strong 216
              (2001), no. 1, 215–241;
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0003188"} hep-th/0003188
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1748177"} MR2002f:81071
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/PL00005547"} doi
          %p An introduction and review to the Hopf-algebraic description of renormalization is in
          %ul
            %li
              Christian Brouder,
              %em Quantum field theory meets Hopf algebra
              (
              %a{:href => "http://de.arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0611153"}> arxiv:hep-th/0611153
              )
          %p A textbook treatment is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"}> Dirk Kreimer
              ,
              %em Knots and Fenyman diagrams
              , Cambridge Lecture Notes in Physics. 13. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.
            %li
              Joseph C. Varilly,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The interface of noncommutative geometry and physics
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0206007"} hep-th/0206007
          %p Some heavywheight automated computations using this formalism are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              D. J. Broadhurst,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"} Dirk Kreimer
              %em Renormalization automated by Hopf algebra
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9810087"}> arXiv:hep-th/9810087
              )
          %p See also
          %ul#Suijlekom
            %li
              W. van Suijlekom,
              %em Representing Feynman graphs on BV-algebras
              , (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0807.0999"}> arXiv
              )
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"}> Dirk Kreimer
              , Karen Yeats,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Diffeomorphisms of quantum fields
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01837"} arxiv/1610.01837
          %h3#ReferencesInBVFormalism In BV formalism
          %p
            Discussion of renormalization in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            is for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic field theory
            in the rigorous framework of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            due to
          %ul
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in the functional approach to classical field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5112"} arXiv:1101.5112
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5232"} arXiv:1110.5232
            %li#Rejzner11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
                ,
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in locally covariant field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5130"}> arXiv:1111.5130
                )
            %li#Rejzner13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
                ,
                %em Remarks on local symmetry invariance in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.7037"}> arXiv:1301.7037
                )
          %p and surveyed in
          %ul
            %li#Rejzner16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"}> Kasia Rejzner
              , section 7 of
              %em Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
              Springer 2016 (
              %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-319-25901-7"}> web
              )
          %p
            Discussion for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+field+theory"} Euclidean field theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Costello11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Renormalization+and+Effective+Field+Theory"} Renormalization and Effective Field Theory
              AMS (2011) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/publications/authors/books/postpub/surv-170"}> publisher webpage
              )
          %p building on
          %ul
            %li#Costello
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
              ,
              %em Renormalisation and the Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1533"}> arXiv:0706.1533
              )
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra+of+observables"} factorization algebra of observables
          %p A vaguely related approach earlier appeared in
          %ul
            %li#Tamarkin
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Tamarkin"}> Dmitry Tamarkin
              ,
              %em A formalism for the renormalization procedure
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312219"}> arXiv:math/0312219
              )
          %h3#ReferencesOnCompactifiedConfigurationSpaces On compactified configuration spaces
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+field+theory"} Euclidean field theory
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions+of+several+variables"} distributions of several variables
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            at (in particular) coincident points, one may
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pullback
            these distributions to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fulton-MacPherson+compactifications"} Fulton-MacPherson compactifications
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces"} configuration spaces
            of points and study renormalization in that perspective.
          %p
            This approach was originally considered specifically for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#AxelrodSinger93
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Axelrod"}> Scott Axelrod
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isadore+Singer"} Isadore Singer
              = succeed "," do
                %em Chern–Simons Perturbation Theory II
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9304087"} arXiv:hep-th/9304087
          %p which was re-amplified in
          %ul
            %li#BottCattaneo97
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raoul+Bott"}> Raoul Bott
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"} Alberto Cattaneo
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Integral invariants of 3-manifolds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/dg-ga/9710001"} arXiv:dg-ga/9710001
            %li#CattaneoMnev10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Mnev"} Pavel Mnev
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Remarks on Chern-Simons invariants
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.2045"} arXiv:0811.2045
          %p
            A systematic development of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            from this perspective is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#BergbauerBrunettiKreimer09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Bergbauer"}> Christoph Bergbauer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"} Romeo Brunetti
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"} Dirk Kreimer
                %em Renormalization and resolution of singularities
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0633"}> arXiv:0908.0633
                )
            %li#Bergbauer09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Bergbauer"}> Christoph Bergbauer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Renormalization and resolution of singularities
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.emg.uni-mainz.de/Dateien/bergbauer.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Berghoff14a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marko+Berghoff"}> Marko Berghoff
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Wonderful renormalization
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www2.mathematik.hu-berlin.de/%7Ekreimer/wp-content/uploads/Berghoff-Marko.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.18452/17160"} doi:10.18452/17160
            %li#Berghoff14b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marko+Berghoff"}> Marko Berghoff
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Wonderful compactifications in quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5583"} arXiv:1411.5583
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes+on+compactified+configuration+spaces+of+points"} Feynman amplitudes on compactified configuration spaces of points
          %h3#operadic_description Operadic description
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Loday"}> Jean-Louis Loday
              , Nikolay M. Nikolov,
              %em Operadic construction of the renormalization group
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1202.1206"}> arXiv:1202.1206
              )
          %h3#relations_to_motives_polylogarithms_positivity Relations to motives, polylogarithms, positivity
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Goncharov"}> Alexander Goncharov
                , Marcus Spradlin, C. Vergu, Anastasia Volovich,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Classical polylogarithms for amplitudes and Wilson loops
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1006.5703"} arxiv/1006.5703
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nima+Arkani-Hamed"}> Nima Arkani-Hamed
                , Jacob L. Bourjaily, Freddy Cachazo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Goncharov"} Alexander Goncharov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Scattering amplitudes and the positive Grassmannian
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1212.5605"} arxiv/1212.5605
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spencer+Bloch"}> Spencer Bloch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%A9l%C3%A8ne+Esnault"} Hélène Esnault
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+Kreimer"} Dirk Kreimer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On motives associated to graph polynomials
                %strong 267
                (2006) 181-225
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0510011"} math.AG/0510011
                %a{:href => "http:&&dx.doi.org/10.1007/s00220-006-0040-2"} doi
        .revisedby
